Overlay patch 0050: skip MK64's opaque screen fill in VR world frames.

R0 finding 3 read the symptom right — the diorama sits in a black rectangle
instead of in the room — but only half the cause. Clearing the eye
framebuffers to alpha 0 (0044) is necessary and not sufficient: MK64 paints
its own full-screen opaque black quad over them a moment later.
`clear_framebuffer(0)` runs from the TOP of the game loop, every frame, in
every game state (main.c, just before game_state_handler), and Fast3D
renders it as a real full-screen fill — alpha 1, depth untouched. Measured,
not guessed: the R1 blit diagnostics (`vr set dbg 2` / `dbg 3`) show engine
depth still at the clear value across the whole background while the alpha
channel is 1 everywhere, which is exactly the signature of a fill drawn with
depth writes off.

In VR world frames the fill has no job to do — the engine already cleared
the eye framebuffer at the head of the pass, and what the fill would erase
is precisely the passthrough we want to keep. Guarded on gSohVRWorldActive,
so flat, 3D-panel and the VR panel fallback all keep the stock clear.

Patched in the function rather than at the call site so every caller is
covered by construction (there are five, across menus, racing and the
credits) and no future one can slip past.


--- a/src/main.c
+++ b/src/main.c
@@ -459,6 +459,18 @@
 // clear_frame_buffer from SM64, with a few edits
 //! @todo Why did void* work for matching
 void* clear_framebuffer(s32 color) {
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    /* SOH_IOS (overlay 0050): in VR world frames the eye framebuffer was
+     * already cleared — to alpha 0 (overlay 0044) — at the head of the pass,
+     * and this full-screen opaque fill would paint the room back out again.
+     * Every other mode keeps the stock clear. */
+    {
+        extern volatile int gSohVRWorldActive;
+        if (gSohVRWorldActive) {
+            return NULL;
+        }
+    }
+#endif
     gDPPipeSync(gDisplayListHead++);
 
     gDPSetRenderMode(gDisplayListHead++, G_RM_OPA_SURF, G_RM_OPA_SURF2);
